            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "self_retaining_retractor_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "2. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/self_retaining_retractor_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "gulmahersurgico.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact parts depend on the model, but a typical ratchet-type retractor may have:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Retractor arms — extend from the central joint towards the operative field.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blades or prongs — engage and retract the tissues.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Joint or hinge — permits the arms to open and close.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ratchet mechanism — locks the arms at selected degrees of separation.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Release mechanism — disengages the ratchet so the instrument can be opened or removed.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Adjustment mechanism — where present, allows further control of the spread or position.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not every self-retaining retractor has all these components. Frame-based systems, for example, may use adjustable blades attached to a supporting frame rather than a simple pair of ratcheted arms."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
