        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "self_retaining_retractor_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "4. Uses and clinical applications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Self-retaining retractors are useful when the operative field must remain open for a sustained period, particularly during procedures requiring both the surgeon's hands for dissection, suturing or other operative tasks."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_6",
              "group_title": "General uses",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain separation of wound edges.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Provide sustained exposure of deeper structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reduce the need for an assistant to hold a handheld retractor continuously.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improve access during prolonged operations.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Support exposure in selected abdominal, thoracic, orthopaedic and other surgical procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_7",
              "group_title": "Applications by instrument",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Instrument",
                    "Examples of use"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Weitlaner",
                      "Superficial soft-tissue operations, selected orthopaedic procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Gelpi",
                      "Small or focused operative fields, including selected perineal and orthopaedic procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Balfour",
                      "Laparotomy, bowel surgery and other open abdominal procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bookwalter",
                      "Major abdominal and pelvic operations requiring adjustable retraction"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Finochietto",
                      "Thoracotomy and rib spreading"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "self_retaining_retractor_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "5. Principles of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/self_retaining_retractor_16.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "gulmahersurgico.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A self-retaining retractor works by applying a controlled outward force to the tissues bordering an operative field and then maintaining that separation through its locking or supporting mechanism."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_9",
              "group_title": "Safe application",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Select the correct type and size. Match the instrument to the depth, width and location of the operative field.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the mechanism. Confirm that the ratchet, hinge, release mechanism or frame functions properly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Position the blades carefully. Ensure that the blades or prongs engage the intended tissues and do not trap vulnerable structures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Open gradually. Increase separation only as much as required for adequate exposure.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Lock securely. Ensure that the mechanism holds the selected position without slipping.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reassess tissue tension. Check that retraction is not excessive and that the operative field remains adequately perfused.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Release carefully. Disengage the locking mechanism before removing the instrument to avoid sudden tissue movement or injury.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key principle: Self-retaining does not mean self-adjusting to tissue safety. The operator must still monitor pressure, tissue tension and the adequacy of exposure."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "self_retaining_retractor_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "6. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Frees the assistant's hands: An assistant can help with suction, instruments or other tasks.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintains sustained exposure: Useful during operations requiring prolonged access.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Provides adjustable retraction: Many designs allow the opening to be increased or decreased.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improves workflow: Can reduce the need for repeated manual repositioning.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Useful in deep or wide operative fields: Frame-based systems can support several blades simultaneously.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "self_retaining_retractor_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "7. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Can exert excessive pressure if opened too widely.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May cause tissue ischaemia or pressure injury during prolonged use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Some models have sharp prongs that can puncture or tear tissue.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May obstruct the operative field if poorly positioned.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ratchets, hinges and frame components can make cleaning and inspection more demanding.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires appropriate selection, positioning and release; it is not suitable for every wound or anatomical site.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "self_retaining_retractor_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "8. Complications and prevention",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Cause",
                    "Prevention"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Skin or soft-tissue tears",
                      "Excessive opening or prongs placed incorrectly",
                      "Select appropriate prongs and apply gradual, controlled tension"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tissue ischaemia",
                      "Excessive or prolonged compression",
                      "Use the minimum effective retraction and reassess periodically"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Nerve injury",
                      "Compression or stretching of nearby nerves",
                      "Identify vulnerable structures and avoid excessive traction"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Vascular injury",
                      "Blade pressure or inadvertent entrapment of vessels",
                      "Position blades carefully and monitor the operative field"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Visceral injury",
                      "Poor placement of abdominal blades or excessive pressure",
                      "Confirm the location of underlying organs before increasing retraction"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Sudden loss of exposure",
                      "Locking mechanism slips or fails",
                      "Inspect the mechanism before use and verify secure engagement"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Instrument-related injury during removal",
                      "Ratchet released abruptly or instrument pulled out while locked",
                      "Release the mechanism deliberately before removal"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "self_retaining_retractor_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "9. Self-retaining versus handheld retractors",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "self_retaining_retractor_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Self-retaining",
                    "Handheld"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "How it stays in position",
                      "Lock, frame or adjustable mechanism",
                      "Continuous manual holding"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Assistant's hands",
                      "Freed for other tasks",
                      "Usually occupied holding the retractor"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Retraction adjustment",
                      "Maintained mechanically once set",
                      "Continuously controlled by the holder"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Risk",
                      "Excessive sustained pressure may go unnoticed",
                      "Pressure can be adjusted immediately by the holder, but fatigue may affect control"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Examples",
                      "Weitlaner, Gelpi, Balfour",
                      "Deaver, Langenbeck, Volkmann"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Neither category is universally superior. The choice depends on the operation, anatomy, duration and required degree of control."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
